 ## Overview
-This method implements the geometry-intensive portion of the volume rendering pipeline—specifically ray generation, perspective transformation, and volume data resampling—using dedicated hardware accelerators rather than general-purpose CPUs or programmable shaders [S1]. It solves the bottleneck of computing sampling locations and interpolating voxel values for millions of rays per second, which traditionally consumes the majority of rendering time in software implementations [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on 3D texture mapping or pixel-based shading, this approach uses a specialized "Geometry Unit" to pre-calculate interpolation weights and perform backward-mapping resampling, feeding dense sample data to subsequent processing stages [S1, S2].
+This method implements the geometry-intensive portion of the volume rendering pipeline—specifically perspective transformation, gradient estimation, and volume data resampling—using dedicated hardware rather than general-purpose processors. It solves the bottleneck where software implementations consume up to 80% of computation time on these operations [S1]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Hardware-Accelerated Volume Rendering via 3D Texturing](2799-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering-via-3d-texturing.md) by its explicit use of precalculated interpolation weights and dedicated gradient hardware (Sobel filters) within a custom geometry unit, rather than relying on standard texture sampling units [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on a per-scanline basis, generating ray parameters and resampling the volume dataset into a rotated coordinate system suitable for ray casting [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** Ray starting points and vectors to the next resample location for each scanline; original volume dataset (voxel values); density lookup table (LUT).
+*   **Output:** Resampled density values and estimated X/Y gradient components for the rotated dataset, passed to the Ray-Casting Unit [S1].
 
